From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:26:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] share GPU buffers as blob resources

A classic 3D resource lives inside the context that created it and has no
handle the renderer can export, so a second context cannot open it. The
Vulkan driver imports gralloc buffers exactly that way, and every import
failed here with "invalid res_id" because the allocator had created a
classic resource.

The driver only considered a blob when host_gbm_enabled was set, which it
derives from whether the host implements NV12 natively. That probe asks
whether the host allocator is minigbm; it says nothing about whether blob
resources work, and a host with a different allocator fails it while
supporting blobs perfectly well. Allocate a blob for any buffer the GPU
shares, and leave the probe gating only the rules that really describe the
other host. The caller has already established that the device offers blob
resources and a host-visible window.

blob_flags_from_use_flags() sets VIRTGPU_BLOB_FLAG_USE_CROSS_DEVICE on
every blob unconditionally, with a comment saying the wider blob support
should revisit it. The kernel implements that flag with the optional
VIRTIO_GPU_F_RESOURCE_UUID feature and rejects the ioctl with EINVAL when
the device does not offer it, so on this host every blob allocation failed
before it reached the renderer -- including the host format probe whose
failure is what cleared host_gbm_enabled.

Query the flag the kernel already exports as VIRTGPU_PARAM_CROSS_DEVICE
and set it only when the device supports it. Mesa gates its own blobs the
same way. Nothing in this product shares gralloc buffers with another
virtio device; the Vulkan driver imports them through prime on the same
device, which needs no uuid.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 virtgpu_virgl.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/virtgpu_virgl.c b/virtgpu_virgl.c
index 17a70c5e44cf166ac08cae1cbd54103e3bed4f62..c99db486d39185640c82187fac7ed76c3a2c2865 100644
--- a/virtgpu_virgl.c
+++ b/virtgpu_virgl.c
@@ -780,9 +780,13 @@ static uint32_t blob_flags_from_use_flags(uint32_t use_flags)
 	if (use_flags & (BO_USE_SW_MASK | BO_USE_GPU_DATA_BUFFER))
 		blob_flags |= VIRTGPU_BLOB_FLAG_USE_MAPPABLE;
 
-	// For now, all blob use cases are cross device. When we add wider
-	// support for blobs, we can revisit making this unconditional.
-	blob_flags |= VIRTGPU_BLOB_FLAG_USE_CROSS_DEVICE;
+	// Cross-device sharing lets another virtio device (virtio-wl, virtio-video)
+	// import the buffer, and the kernel implements it with the optional
+	// VIRTIO_GPU_F_RESOURCE_UUID feature. A device that does not offer that
+	// feature rejects every blob carrying the flag with EINVAL, so ask for it
+	// only when the kernel reports it, exactly as Mesa does for its own blobs.
+	if (params[param_cross_device].value)
+		blob_flags |= VIRTGPU_BLOB_FLAG_USE_CROSS_DEVICE;
 
 	return blob_flags;
 }
@@ -973,7 +977,27 @@ static bool should_use_blob(struct driver *drv, uint32_t format, uint64_t use_fl
 {
 	struct virgl_priv *priv = (struct virgl_priv *)drv->priv;
 
-	// Only use blob when host gbm is available
+	/*
+	 * Only a blob resource carries a handle the renderer can hand to a
+	 * second context, so a buffer the GPU shares has to be one: the Vulkan
+	 * driver imports gralloc buffers that way, and a classic 3D resource
+	 * has nothing to export. The caller already established that the
+	 * device offers blob resources and a host-visible window.
+	 *
+	 * A buffer the guest also reads or writes itself stays a classic
+	 * resource: its contents reach the guest through transfers, which a
+	 * blob backed by renderer-owned storage does not serve.
+	 */
+	if (!(use_flags & BO_USE_SW_MASK) &&
+	    (use_flags & (BO_USE_RENDERING | BO_USE_SCANOUT | BO_USE_TEXTURE)))
+		return true;
+
+	/*
+	 * The remaining rules describe a host whose allocator is minigbm, which
+	 * this driver detects by asking whether the host implements YUV natively
+	 * rather than emulating it. That probe says nothing about whether blob
+	 * resources work, so it stays where it is and gates only its own rules.
+	 */
 	if (!priv->host_gbm_enabled)
 		return false;
 
